---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import SkillGroup from '../components/SkillGroup.astro';
import { skillGroups } from '../data/skills';
import { profile } from '../data/profile';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Camilo Aponte: twenty years of writing and documentation experience, now building AI-ready knowledge infrastructure — enterprise Fin AI integrations, a 2,056-article knowledge base, and the systems that keep both accurate."
>
  <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-content px-6 py-14 md:px-8">
    <img
      src="/images/camilo-headshot.jpg"
      alt="Portrait of Camilo Aponte"
      width="160"
      height="160"
      class="absolute right-6 top-10 hidden h-32 w-32 rounded-md border border-border-subtle object-cover shadow-card sm:block md:right-8 md:h-44 md:w-44"
    />
    <SectionHeading eyebrow="About" heading="Camilo Aponte" level="h1" />

    <div class="prose-body mt-3">
      <p>
        Twenty years of professional writing and documentation experience came before this work — eighteen in
        journalism, language education, and publishing, two in localization QA — and the last fourteen months have
        gone into building AI-ready knowledge infrastructure. I spent almost two decades explaining things for a living before
        I spent any of it writing code: as a radio journalist at Caracol Radio, a university Spanish lecturer, and a
        certified DELE examiner for the Instituto Cervantes. That work was really one skill applied three ways —
        figuring out what an audience actually needs to understand something, in what order, with how much context.
        It turned out to be almost exactly the skill a knowledge base needs when the reader is a language model
        instead of a person.
      </p>

      <p>
        I joined Search Atlas in June 2025 to build an AI Automation function that didn't yet exist. There was no AI
        governance model, no production connectors between the support chatbot and the company's internal services,
        and a 250-article knowledge base written for humans reading top to bottom rather than for a model retrieving
        one specific answer. Fourteen months later, that chatbot — Fin AI — resolves roughly 70% of conversations
        without a human, up from about 30%, and the knowledge base behind it holds 2,056 structured, AI-ready
        articles.
      </p>

      <p><strong>The work that got it there:</strong></p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Led the integration of Fin AI across 12 internal services and 11 engineering teams, authoring the V1
          Canonical Runtime Schema — the shared data contract every one of the 19 live Data Connectors and 17
          operational Procedures had to implement.
        </li>
        <li>
          Redesigned the conversational architecture from first principles: expanded the Guidance Prompt library
          from 0 to 78 and took chatbot resolution from ~30% to ~70% in under two months.
        </li>
        <li>
          Expanded the production knowledge base from 250 to 2,056 structured articles through a pipeline that
          generated 1,687 AI-assisted drafts, published 1,320 improvements, and closed 331 identified knowledge gaps
          — while a companion governance system removed 446 duplicate articles and audited 649 Guidance issues.
        </li>
        <li>
          Built the surrounding automation layer — knowledge-gap detection, conversation recovery, documentation
          governance, browser-based UI auditing, and operational reporting — using Claude Code, Python, Playwright,
          SQLite, and n8n.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Outside of AI engineering, I'm the author of "Lee en español," founder and editor of two Spanish-language
        learning magazines, and a former university lecturer on Spanish as a foreign language across Colombia and
        Poland. That background is why I default to treating documentation and prompts as editorial problems first
        — clarity, structure, and audience — before I treat them as engineering problems.
      </p>

      <p>
        I'm currently going deeper on LangChain, LangGraph, advanced Retrieval-Augmented Generation, vector
        databases (Qdrant, Pinecone, Weaviate), and formal LLM evaluation — the next layer needed to keep pushing
        resolution rate and knowledge quality past where prompt and knowledge-base work alone can take them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100">Languages</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-10 gap-y-3">
        {
          profile.languages.map((language) => (
            <div>
              <dt class="text-sm text-ink-100">{language.name}</dt>
              <dd class="text-xs text-ink-400">{language.level}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100">Capabilities</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
        {skillGroups.map((group) => <SkillGroup title={group.title} skills={group.skills} />)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
